# Bug Report
 🔎 Search Terms
 - generic type
 - variadic elements
 - array
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
<!-- When did you start seeing this bug occur?
"Bugs" that have existed in TS for a long time are very likely to be FAQs; refer to
 If possible, please try testing the nightly version of TS to see if it's already been fixed.
For npm: `typescript@next`
This is also the 'Nightly' version in the playground: Note: The TypeScript Playground can be used to try older versions of TypeScript.
Please keep and fill in the line that best applies:
-->
- This is the behavior in every version I tried (including v4,Nightly)(in v3 everything errors), and I reviewed the FAQ for entries about "Generics"
 ⏯ Playground Link
[Playground link with relevant code](
 💻 Code
ts
class I<SS extends string>{
 f() {
 let x: [ //Errors when SS is generic and `t` exists
 t: "AAA",
 ...args: {
 [S in SS]: [a: number];
 }[SS]
 ] = ["AAA", 1];
 type SS1 = string;
 let y: [ // OK when SS1 is not generic
 t: "AAA",
 ...args: {
 [S in SS1]: [a: number];
 }[SS1]
 ] = ["AAA", 1];
 type SS2 = "1" | "2" | "3";
 let z: [ // OK when SS2 is not generic
 t: "AAA",
 ...args: {
 [S in SS2]: [a: number];
 }[SS2]
 ] = ["AAA", 1];
 let w: [ // OK when `t` doesn't exist!!!
 ...args: {
 [S in SS]: [a: number];
 }[SS]
 ] = [1];
 }
}
 🙁 Actual behavior
`x` errors, but `w` doesn't
`x` errors, but `y` and `z` doesn't
 🙂 Expected behavior
`x` shouldn't be a error.

