description:
#51234 introduced a new `@overload` tag, but it doesn't have the same type-checking rules as TypeScript's overloads.
Namely that we ensure that for each overload, the implementation signature is assignable to that overload, allowing the return type to be bidirectionally assignable. If we want the same results in `checkJs`, then the following should error:
js
// @ts-check
/**
 * @overload
 * @param {number} x
 * @returns {number}
 */
/**
 * @param {string} x
 * @returns {string}
 */
function wut(x) {
 return String(x);
}
wut(123).toFixed(2);

text:
Bug Title: `@overload` doesn't check implementation against overload signatures
Bug Description: #51234 introduced a new `@overload` tag, but it doesn't have the same type-checking rules as TypeScript's overloads.
Namely that we ensure that for each overload, the implementation signature is assignable to that overload, allowing the return type to be bidirectionally assignable. If we want the same results in `checkJs`, then the following should error:
js
// @ts-check
/**
 * @overload
 * @param {number} x
 * @returns {number}
 */
/**
 * @param {string} x
 * @returns {string}
 */
function wut(x) {
 return String(x);
}
wut(123).toFixed(2);

description:
# Bug Report
 🔎 Search Terms
declaration emit this any
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
- This is the behavior in every version I tried
 ⏯ Playground Link
[Playground link with relevant code](
 💻 Code
ts
export function test() {
 return {
 relyingOnThis() {
 return this;
 },
 };
}
// seen and recognized locally in TS files
test().relyingOnThis();
 🙁 Actual behavior
ts
// generated .d.ts
export declare function test(): {
 relyingOnThis(): any;
};
 🙂 Expected behavior
Make `this` type to work in such contexts and emit that:
ts
declare function test(): {
 relyingOnThis(): this; // currently reports `A 'this' type is available only in a non-static member of a class or interface.(2526)`
};
Or emit the content of the anonymous type there somehow (when possible, be cautious with circularities, generics etc):
ts
declare const _type: {
 relyingOnThis(): typeof _type;
}
declare function test(): typeof _type
Or just emit a diagnostic that this isn't portable and that an explicit annotation is needed.

