class A<T extends number = number> {
 value!: T;
 child!: InstanceType<typeof A.B<A<T>>>
 static B = class B<T extends A> {
 parent!: T;
 } }
var a = new A
a.child.parent.value
// ^? number

description:
# lib Update Request
<!--
 Please fill in each section completely. Thank you!
 Are you here for one of these commonly-requested lib changes?
 * Object.keys - see * Array methods - see * parseInt, parseFloat, isFinite, isNaN, etc. - see The DOM lib is maintained elsewhere and you can skip a step by filing issues/PRs for the DOM at that repo.
 See -->
## Configuration Check
<!--
 If you're missing common new methods like Array.includes, you may have a misconfigured project.
 Try setting `lib: "es2020"` and checking whether the type you want is present.
 You can diagnose further by running `tsc` with `--listFilesOnly` or `--showConfig`.
 Conversely, if you are seeing built-in methods you expect to *not* see, check your 'lib' setting
 or review your dependencies for lib/reference directives that might be polluting
 your global scope. This is common when using the 'node' type library. See Replace the text below:
-->
My compilation *target* is `ES2022` and my *lib* is `ES2022`.
## Missing / Incorrect Definition
TypedPropertyDescriptor<T> behaves unexpectedly when passing in a function with a union return type.
I have created a minimal reproduction in ts playground to show this behaviour. I would expect in the snippet below that the method I am decorating would fall within the constraints of the type passed to the generic however it does not. The method must absolutely return a type that is Promise<void | Message> which doesn't make much sense.
## Sample Code
typescript
type Message = { Body: string };
type Test = (message: Message) => Promise<void | Message>;
const test: Test = async (message: Message) => ({ Body: 'hey' });
// ^ This works as it should
const a = (target: object, propertyKey: string | symbol, descriptor: TypedPropertyDescriptor<Test>) => {
 const og = descriptor.value;
 console.log('Hey')
};
class A {
 @a
 // ^ This does not. TypeScript complains when the return type clearly falls within the union
 public async method(message: Message) {
 return message;
 }
}
[TypeScript Playground](

