description:
# Bug Report
<!--
 Please fill in each section completely. Thank you!
-->
 🔎 Search Terms
definition file, javascript, generated, incorrect, wrong, constructor, override, expected, 1005
<!--
 What search terms did you use when trying to find an existing bug report?
 List them here so people in the future can find this one more easily.
-->
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
- This is the behavior in every version I tried, and I reviewed the FAQ for entries about definition generation from JavaScript
 - I tested from 3.5.1 to Nightly on the TypeScript Playground
 ⏯ Playground Link
[Playground link of the JavaScript code that generates the bad definition](
[Playground link with the generated definition code showing the error](
 💻 Code
<!-- Please post the relevant code sample here as well-->
**JavaScript code:**
js
class MyClass {
 /**
 * @param {any} someAnyArgument (In my real-world example this `any` is implicit)
 */
 someMethod(someAnyArgument) {
 this.constructor = someAnyArgument;
 }
}
**Definition code generated by TypeScript from the above JavaScript:**
ts
declare class MyClass {
 /**
 * @param {any} someAnyArgument (In my real-world example this `any` is implicit)
 */
 someMethod(someAnyArgument: any): void;
 constructor: any;
 // ^ '(' expected.(1005)
}
 🙁 Actual behavior
TypeScript generates an invalid `constructor` property of type `any` on the class definition.
 🙂 Expected behavior
TypeScript should not generate any property in this class. While it might notice that there is a statement that sets `this.constructor` to a value it should ignore it when generating property definitions for the class because its type is already implicitly a method with no parameters and it is illegal to even define a `constructor` class member in this way.
 Extra info
Just for extra info, here is the same program written in TypeScript directly as well as the `.d.ts` definition produced from that TypeScript:
**TypeScript code:**
ts
class MyClass {
 someMethod(someAnyArgument: any) {
 this.constructor = someAnyArgument;
 }
}
[Playground](
**Generated definition:**
ts
declare class MyClass {
 someMethod(someAnyArgument: any): void;
}
As you can see, the expected valid definition file is generated.

