Bug Title: JSDoc @extends doesn't work with multiple lines
Bug Description: <!-- ⚠️⚠️ Do Not Delete This! bug_report_template ⚠️⚠️ -->
<!-- Please read our Rules of Conduct: -->
<!-- 🕮 Read our guide about submitting issues: -->
<!-- 🔎 Search existing issues to avoid creating duplicates. -->
<!-- 🧪 Test using the latest Insiders build to see if your issue has already been fixed: -->
<!-- 💡 Instead of creating your report here, use 'Report Issue' from the 'Help' menu in VS Code to pre-fill useful information. -->
<!-- 🔧 Launch with `code --disable-extensions` to check. -->
Does this issue occur when all extensions are disabled?: Yes
<!-- 🪓 If you answered No above, use 'Help: Start Extension Bisect' from Command Palette to try to identify the cause. -->
<!-- 📣 Issues caused by an extension need to be reported directly to the extension publisher. The 'Help > Report Issue' dialog can assist with this. -->
Version: 1.77.0 (user setup)
Commit: 7f329fe6c66b0f86ae1574c2911b681ad5a45d63
Date: Electron: 19.1.11
Chromium: 102.0.5005.196
Node.js: 16.14.2
V8: 10.2.154.26-electron.0
OS: Windows_NT x64 10.0.19044
Sandboxed: Yes
Steps to Reproduce:
js
// @ts-check
// it is possible to define multiline types
/**
 * @typedef {{
 * a: number | string;
 * b: boolean | string[];
 * }} MyType
 */
/**
 * @template {MyType} BaseType
 */
class Test {
 /**
 * @param {BaseType} a
 */
 constructor(a) {
 return a
 }
}
// but, when using "@extends"
// you're forced to do everything in one line, or you get errors
// no errors
// and in the call, it shows the type defined here
// autocomplete works as expected
/**
 * @extends {Test<{a: string, b: string[]}>}
 */
class Implement0 extends Test {}
// this shows the correct error
// Type '{ a: string; b: string; }' does not satisfy the constraint 'MyType'.
// and in the call, it shows the type defined here
// autocomplete works as expected
/**
 * @extends {Test<{a: string, b: string}>}
 */
class Implement1 extends Test {}
// this one shows the error
// Type '{}' does not satisfy the constraint 'MyType'.
// it ignores the rest, and there's no autocomplete
/**
 * @extends {Test<{
 * a: string,
 * b: string[]
 * }>}
 */
class Implement2 extends Test {}
// autocomplete ok
new Implement0({a: 'a', b: ['b']})
// autocomplete works with the defined type (even if the type is not correct)
new Implement1({a: 'a', b: 'b'})
// type "{}"
new Implement2({})

