description:
# Bug Report
<!--
 Please fill in each section completely. Thank you!
-->
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
<!-- When did you start seeing this bug occur?
"Bugs" that have existed in TS for a long time are very likely to be FAQs; refer to
 If possible, please try testing the nightly version of TS to see if it's already been fixed.
For npm: `typescript@next`
This is also the 'Nightly' version in the playground: Note: The TypeScript Playground can be used to try older versions of TypeScript.
Please keep and fill in the line that best applies:
-->
- v4.8.4
 💻 Code
<!-- Please post the relevant code sample here as well-->
ts
// modules.ts
export const someEnv: string | undefined = process.env.TEST;
export const isString = (v: any): v is string => {
 if (typeof v "string") {
 return true;
 }
 return false;
};
if (isString(someEnv)) {
 console.log(someEnv); // <- "`someEnv` type is string" works fine here.
}
![스크린샷 2023-04-21 오후 1 31 26](
ts
// app.ts
import { someEnv, isString } from "./modules.ts";
if (isString(someEnv)) {
 console.log(someEnv); // <- The `someEnv` type must be a `string`, but in this case, it is displayed as `string | undefined`
}
![스크린샷 2023-04-21 오후 1 31 54](
- When the value passed as an argument is in the same file as the TypeGuard function, it can infer types correctly, but when the value passed as an argument is a variable from another file, it seems that type inference is not performed correctly.
 🙁 Actual behavior
- If the result of executing the isString function is true, then the type of someEnv should be string, but it is being displayed as string | undefined.
 🙂 Expected behavior
- If the result of executing the isString function is true, then the type of someEnv should be string, and it should be displayed as string.

