description:
# Bug Report
 🔎 Search Terms
"Type checking arrow function bug," "typescript callback this bug,"
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
- This is the behavior in every version I tried, and I reviewed the FAQ for entries about "this," proposals
 ⏯ Playground Link
[Playground link with relevant code](
 💻 Code
<!-- Please post the relevant code sample here as well-->
ts
type WrongObject = {value: number};
type CorrectObject = {name: string};
function isCorrect(obj: any): obj is CorrectObject {
 return obj.name;
}
function callsCallback(cb: (name: string)=>void): void {
 cb('test');
}
function problemFunction(this: CorrectObject | WrongObject): void {
 //check type
 if (!isCorrect(this)) return;
 console.log(this.name) //this works fine, as we would expected
 callsCallback((name)=>{
 this.name = name; //This throws error, even though "this" should be bound by the arrow function, where it's specified to be the correct type
 });
}
 🙁 Actual behavior
After correctly narrowing the type of `this` using type coercion (I think that's the proper term) it works correctly in the local scope, but an arrow function which is declared in the same scope won't know what `this` is. Replacing the `this: CorrectObject | WrongObject` argument in `problemFunction` with `myValue: CorrectObject | WrongObject` works as expected, it's only broken when using `this`.
 🙂 Expected behavior
Arrow functions bind `this` in the scope which they're declared, therefore if an object is determined to be of a certain type above where the arrow function is declared, and in the same scope, the arrow function should inherently know what `this` is.

