description:
# Bug Report
<!--
 Please fill in each section completely. Thank you!
-->
If you do all of these things:
- use CJS modules
- use experimental decorators
- emit decorator metadata
- import a class or something from another module, but only use it as a type on a method
- decorate _arguments_ of that method
- DO NOT decorate any classes or methods that leverage that imported type
Then you will get a runtime error because the compiler emits code that references the required module, but it won't emit the code that requires the module.
 🔎 Search Terms
<!--
 What search terms did you use when trying to find an existing bug report?
 List them here so people in the future can find this one more easily.
-->
decorator argument, runtime error
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
<!-- When did you start seeing this bug occur?
I've seen this in every version of TS I tried.
 ⏯ Playground Link
<!--
 A link to a TypeScript Playground "Share" link which shows this behavior.
 This should have the same code as the code snippet below, and use whichever settings are relevant to your report.
 As a last resort, you can link to a repo, but these will be slower for us to investigate.
-->
[Playground link with relevant code](
 💻 Code
ts
// Any import that you're only going to use as a "type" below
// (but don't "import type")
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
function whatever(a: any, b: any, c: any) {}
class Test {
 // ONLY decorate arguments in the whole file!!!
 foo(
 @whatever arg1: string,
 @whatever arg2: number
 ): Observable<string> {
 return null!;
 }
}
 🙁 Actual behavior
The output will NOT `const rxjs_1 = require('rxjs')` (or wherever you're importing the type from), but
it will still use `rxjs_1` in the decorator metadata. Causing a runtime error.
"use strict";
var __decorate = (this && this.__decorate) || function (decorators, target, key, desc) {
 var c = arguments.length, r = c < 3 ? target : desc null ? desc = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(target, key) : desc, d;
 if (typeof Reflect "object" && typeof Reflect.decorate "function") r = Reflect.decorate(decorators, target, key, desc);
