description:
🔎 Search Terms
declaration emit triple slash references
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
- This is the behavior in every version I tried, and I reviewed the FAQ ⏯ Playground Link
 💻 Code
ts
// @declaration: true
// @filename: /.src/node_modules/@types/node/index.d.ts
declare module "url" {
 export class Url {}
 export function parse(): Url; }
// @filename: usage.ts
import { parse } from "url";
export const thing:import("url").Url = parse(); //export const thing = parse(); // If type is inferred a /// directive is added
 🙁 Actual behavior
If the type of the variable is inferred a reference directive is added (`/// <reference types="./.src/node_modules/@types/node">`). If the type is written in code, the directive is not added
 🙂 Expected behavior
The directive is either added in both cases or not at all. Additional information about the issue
Discovered while working on isolated declarations. Generally we will need to error if the declarations need to add reference directives if they not already specified in code.

