description:
🔎 Search Terms
const type parameter inference intersection
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
- This is the behavior in every version I tried
 ⏯ Playground Link
 💻 Code
ts
interface Config<T1 extends { type: string }> {
 useIt: T1;
}
declare function test<
 T1 extends { type: string },
 const TConfig extends Config<T1>,
>(config: { produceThing: T1 } & TConfig): TConfig;
const result = test({
 produceThing: {} as {
 type: "foo";
 },
 useIt: {
 type: "foo",
 },
 extra: 10,
});
 🙁 Actual behavior
We can observe an EPC error here which indicates that `TConfig` failed to be inferred.
 🙂 Expected behavior
I would expect this to be inferred successfully. Additional information about the issue
Such an inference candidate is gathered for `TConfig`: `{ produceThing: { type: "foo"; }; useIt: { type: string; }; extra: number; }`. As we can see, `useIt` refers to a `string` type and not to a string literal type. This, in turn, fails the `compareTypes` check since the constraint is `Config<{ type: 'foo' }>` and thus the constraint is assigned as the inferred type.

