Found 1 error in packages/compiler-sfc/src/parse.ts:96
 ELIFECYCLE Command failed with exit code 1.
This is odd, because `createCache` is defined as:
ts
import { LRUCache } from 'lru-cache'
export function createCache<T extends {}>(
 max = 500
): Map<string, T> | LRUCache<string, T> {
 if (__GLOBAL__ || __ESM_BROWSER__) {
 return new Map<string, T>()
 }
 return new LRUCache({ max })
}
A bisect points to #55725, and we unfortunately didn't run the extended suite there. cc @andrewbranch I can fix this with an explicit annotation, but this feels weird.
The funkiness here seems to be that this is a monorepo build of all files in various `/packages/**` dirs, but the output goes into `/temp`, meaning no `d.ts` file's imports resolve properly. Before, the export would get an annotation of `import("lru-cache")`, but now it tries to emit a real import, or something along those lines.

description:
🔎 Search Terms
"Incremental hides errors", "incremental errors", "buildinfo errors"
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
- This changed between versions ? and 4.5, and between versions 4.6 and 4.7
- This is the behavior in every version I tried, and I reviewed the FAQ for entries about incremental, buildinfo, errors
- I was unable to test this on prior versions because my test case doesn't work without moduleResolution set to Node16/NodeNext/Bundler, and 4.4 doesn't support those at all.
In versions 4.5 to 4.6, if you run `npx tsc` thrice (without tsconfig.tsbuildinfo at the start), the first two show the error, but the third runs without that error (it still shows the error about NodeNext being unstable).
In versions 4.7 to 5.3beta, if you run `npx tsc` two times (again without the buildinfo), the first shows the error, but the second and every one after don't.
 ⏯ Playground Link
_No response_
 💻 Code
ts
// @filename: node_modules/ky/package.json
{
 "name": "ky",
 "type": "module",
 "main": "./distribution/index.js"
}
// @filename: node_modules/ky/distribution/index.d.ts
type KyInstance = {
 extend(options: Record<string,unknown>): KyInstance;
}
declare const ky: KyInstance;
export default ky;
// @filename: package.json
{
 "type": "module"
}
// @filename: tsconfig.json
{
 "compilerOptions": {
 "module": "NodeNext",
 "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
 "composite": true,
 "skipLibCheck": true,
 "skipDefaultLibCheck": true
 },
}
// @filename: index.ts
import ky from 'ky';
export const api = ky.extend({});
 🙁 Actual behavior
When running tsc, the "Exported variable 'api' has or is using name 'KyInstance' from external module "node_modules/ky/distribution/index" but cannot be named.ts(4023)" error shows up the first time, but then when you run tsc again, the error dissapears.
 🙂 Expected behavior
I expected that if there's a typescript error, that the typescript error would appear every time I ran the compiler until I fix the error rather than getting swallowed up after the first time.
It should follow the same behavior as other errors: e.g. `const string: string = 6`, or an unused `@ts-expect-error` directive. In those other two cases, the errors continue to show no matter how many times you run tsc.
 Additional information about the issue
_No response_

