const v1 = new GenericObject() as
 GenericObject & ({ a?: string } | { b?: number });
v1.x = 432; // <-- Type 'number' is not assignable to type 'T'.
const v2 = new GenericObject() as
 GenericObject & { a?: string };
v2.x = 42; // works
class GenericObjectWithoutSetter<T = number> {
 declare x: T
}
const v3 = new GenericObjectWithoutSetter() as
 GenericObjectWithoutSetter & ({ a?: string } | { b?: number });
v3.x = 42; // works
class NormalObject {
 set x(x: number) { }
}
const v4 = new NormalObject() as
 NormalObject & ({ a?: string } | { b?: number });
v4.x = 42; // works
 🙁 Actual behavior
TypeScript shows an error:
Type 'number' is not assignable to type 'T'.
 'T' could be instantiated with an arbitrary type which could be unrelated to 'number'.(2322)
 🙂 Expected behavior
The property assignment should be valid. This works with all other type intersections, e.g. `GenericObject & { a?: string }`, just not with union types like `GenericObject & ({ a?: string } | { b?: number })`.
 Additional information about the issue
_No response_

description:
🔎 Search Terms
"An object literal cannot have multiple properties with the same name" object destructure destructuring computed
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
- This changed between versions 5.0.4 and 5.1.6
 ⏯ Playground Link
 💻 Code
ts
function bar(props: {x?: string, y?: string}) {
 const { x = '', y = ''} = props;
 return {
 [x]: 1,
 [y]: 2,
 };
}
ts
function foo({ x = '', y = ''}: {x?: string, y?: string}) {
 return {
 [x]: 1,
 [y]: 2,
 };
}
 🙁 Actual behavior
In the first example - the `[y]: 2` line reports `An object literal cannot have multiple properties with the same name.(1117)`
In the second example - the `[y]: 2` line reports nothing.
 🙂 Expected behavior
There should be no reports as both variables are typed as `string`.
 Additional information about the issue
The above code works fine in [5.0.4]( but fails in both [5.1.6]( and [5.2.2](
It looks like it's been fixed in [5.3.0-beta](
I figured I should file this at least for bookkeeping purposes and just as an FYI considering i don't see any other issues filed about this particular error. Feel free to close this as fixed.

