Bug Title: Runtime error for emitDecoratorMetadata in an (almost) ts.transpileModule() emit
Bug Description: 🔎 Search Terms
emitDecoratorMetadata, error
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
- This is the behavior in every version I tried, and I reviewed the FAQ for entries about v5.2.2, v5.3.2
 ⏯ Playground Link
[Bug Workbench](
 💻 Code
ts
// @experimentalDecorators
// @emitDecoratorMetadata
// @isolatedModules
// @noResolve
// @module: commonjs
import { type MyList } from './types';
declare var property: any;
export class MyElement {
 @property
 myList?: MyList;
}
// @filename: types.d.ts
import { List } from './immutable';
export type MyList = List<number>;
 🙁 Actual behavior
Using a regular emit, this produces:
js
"use strict";
var _a;
Object.defineProperty(exports, "__esModule", { value: true });
exports.MyElement = void 0;
const tslib_1 = require("tslib");
class MyElement {
}
exports.MyElement = MyElement;
tslib_1.__decorate([
 property,
 tslib_1.__metadata("design:type", typeof (_a = typeof types_1.MyList !== "undefined" && types_1.MyList) "function" ? _a : Object)
], MyElement.prototype, "myList", void 0);
Note the `types_1.MyList` reference. But `types_1` is not defined.
 🙂 Expected behavior
Because the import for `MyList` uses `import type`, TypeScript definitely elides the import. The decorator metadata emit should therefore not check if `types_1` exists.
 Additional information about the issue
We're currently adopting `ts.transpileModule()`. However we're not transpiling individual files just yet. Instead we're using `ts.Program#emit` with `noResolve` and few source files for ... reasons.
- If we give TypeScript only the single file, the emit is correct: `tslib_1.__metadata("design:type", Object)`.
- If we give TypeScript all transitive dependencies, the emit is correct: `tslib_1.__metadata("design:type", Object)`
- But in this setup where TypeScript has the immediate dependency but the transitive one is missing, the emit leads to an runtime error.
I could totally understand if you don't support this kind of usage. But I'd be happy if you do :-).

description:
🔎 Search Terms
hasinstance, instanceof, 2359, TS2359
 🕗 Version & Regression Information
- I was unable to test this on prior versions because this is recent functionality introduced in #55052
 ⏯ Playground Link
 💻 Code
ts
type HasInstanceOf = {
 [Symbol.hasInstance](x:unknown): boolean
}
// ok
function myInstanceOf(x:unknown, c:HasInstanceOf){
 return x instanceof c
}
// broken
function myInstanceOfIntersection(x:unknown, c:HasInstanceOf & object){
 return x instanceof c
}
// broken
function myInstanceOfGeneric<C extends HasInstanceOf>(x:unknown, c:C){
 return x instanceof c
}
 🙁 Actual behavior
The second and third examples give error TS2359
> The right-hand side of an 'instanceof' expression must be either of type 'any', a class, function, or other type assignable to the 'Function' interface type, or an object type with a 'Symbol.hasInstance' method.
In all cases, the right-hand side *is* an object type with a `Symbol.hasInstance` method.
 🙂 Expected behavior
I expect all three examples to typecheck.
 Additional information about the issue
_No response_

