description:
Imagine the following file:
ts
// comment
function f() {}
When I press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> on the line containing `f`, and paste it again, what VS Code does is it creates a buffer with the following contents:
ts
// comment
<details><summary>and then sends **this request for paste edits**</summary>
json
 {
 "file": "d:\\scratch\\copypasta\\src\\file.ts",
 "pastedText": [
 "function f() {}\r\n"
 ],
 "pasteLocations": [
 {
 "start": {
 "line": 2,
 "offset": 1
 },
 "end": {
 "line": 2,
 "offset": 1
 }
 }
 ],
 "copiedFrom": {
 "file": "d:\\scratch\\copypasta\\src\\file.ts",
 "spans": [
 {
 "start": {
 "line": 3,
 "offset": 1
 },
 "end": {
 "line": 3,
 "offset": 16
 }
 }
 ]
 }
}
</details>
<details><summary>and receives **this response**.</summary>
json
{
 "edits": [
 {
 "fileName": "d:/scratch/copypasta/src/file.ts",
 "textChanges": [
 {
 "start": {
 "line": 1,
 "offset": 15
 },
 "end": {
 "line": 1,
 "offset": 15
 },
 "newText": "function f() {}\r\n"
 }
 ]
 }
 ],
 "fixId": "providePostPasteEdits"
}
</details>
After the paste, the file no longer contains the original contents, and instead contains invalid text.
ts
// commentfunction f() {}

